A||N||10||Jeff's '86 1/2 GT||Sensor on Air Cleaner?||||5||
Z||000000||Jeff's '86 1/2 GT||04-25-2000||06:56 AM||||<BR>What is the sensor on the V6 Air Cleaner box?  I know if you unplug it you get a check engine light, but what does it do?  My Chilton's Manual has nothing on it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Going cold induction, and need to know if I need to keep it or not?<BR>Thanks for the help.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||04-25-2000||08:48 AM||||yes you need to keep it. it measures incomming air temperature. Now that you've fooled with it and set codes make sure you clear the ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000002||80's BOY||04-25-2000||02:22 PM||||i've heard of an intake temp. sensor relocator kit that fools the engine into thinking it's cold outside for some free hp. alot of mudstains.....i mean..mustangs use this kit to put the sensor away from heat in the engine bay. anyone tried it yet??||||reg||5
Z||000003||Jeff's '86 1/2 GT||04-25-2000||02:59 PM||||Well my cold induction doesn't have a spot to put that senor as I have no air cleaner box any more on the V6, and the car runs so much better this way.  Where do I put it? ||||reg||5
Z||000004||theogre||04-25-2000||03:12 PM||||Fiero already uses cold air intake. Although plumbing and routing of it leaves something to be desired.<P>The sensor doesn't read engine compartment temp. relocating it might work it the V6 since the air in the can should be fairly close to ambient. It sure as heck won't in the 87+ 4 cyls. Air temp in the 4 cyl intake can change over 20F in 2 seconds. I wouldn't mess with it unless you know exactly what it does and why. <P>The sensor meausures incomming air as I said. The ECM wants to know the temperature of the actual air the engine will be eating and will make FA mix adjustments on the fly based on it. Relocating the sensor out of the intake air stream likely isn't going to help HP, and can cause the FA ratio to go way off. Far enough off to cause ECM errors. Posibly far enough off to cause serious ping problems.<P>In the V6 cleaning up the intake plumbing will do allot to make the system look better. Should also make the sensor more responsive to current conditions. If anything I'd move the sensor as close to the throttle body as posible. (The 4 cyl one is in the intake manifold directly.) You want the ECM to read the air temp as close to the plenum as posible so the FA mix will be correct. Remember as the air passes thru the plumbing it will pick up some heat from the engine compartment. (Engine compartment temperatures can be easily 200F. I've measured 150F+ with my 4 cyl on a 70F day.)That heat needs to be accounted for in the ECM calculations.<P>Remember, A tiny bit rich you might make negligable HP gains. Very rich mix you waste gas smoke and likely smell bad. Lean you ping, you burn things. Moving this sensor could have any of the above results depending on how much error you introduce and in which direction it is.<P>One thing you could do that's cheap, and likely looks nice if you take your time with it, is to simply paint the air cleaner and plumbing silver or white to reflect heat. That way the air moving thru won't pick up so much heat from the engine compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jeff's '86 1/2 GT||04-25-2000||04:02 PM||||Thank you for all of your info.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I just talked to ED at the Fiero Factory, and he said that I should just disconect it.  He stated that it won't cause any problems with the V6 being disconected, that the adjustments it makes will be so small that I will never notice them.  But then I will get the light on.  Now when I have to smog the car I plan on putting everything back to stock, that is the only thing that keeps me from remounting the senor.  How do I clear the codes.  My light only comes on after the car has been driven a bit, and then goes off when the car is turned off.  So, when I put everything back to stock, do you think the light will stay off or will I need to clear the codes (I forgot to ask him this one   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> )?  Once again how do I do this?<BR>Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Jeff's '86 1/2 GT (edited 04-25-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000006||DRH||04-25-2000||06:14 PM||||My understanding was that the ECM used the sensor in the air cleaner box to estimate air density since it does not have a "real" air density sensor like some cars. If that is true I would think that it's affect would be minimal as stated by Ed.<P>Maybe try to leave it hooked up but not mounted (tied somewhere safe of course)?<P>The temp sensor is just a variable resistor. You might play with putting a resistor across in it's place to feed the ECM a steady temp. I doubt if the ECM will complain about a steady temp as long as it is in a valid range. There's a link at the bottom of this note that give temp/resistance values. Colder should make the mixture slghtly richer in open loop (accelerating) mode. Too hot MIGHT lean it to the point of pinging depending on how much the ECM actually adjust. The O2 sensor takes over in closed loop (cruising) anyway.<P>When you "correct" the sensor by reconnecting it or by fooling the ECM the light should go off. The error code will still be stored until you clear it (disconnect power) though.<P> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/MAT.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/MAT.html</A> <P><BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||04-25-2000||08:04 PM||||Here is a pic of my intake ( not a good pic) but this is the stock tube with a little  modification <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> with a 6" K&N cone at the end, just at the opening of the side scoop. You can't see it but the sensor is hooked up? It's on the back side of the tube close to the filter. I had to put a mounting hole in the tube to put the  sensor in, When I first did this mod I left it off? with the check engine light on, so I mounted it and it's running fine better than with the stock air box.<P>Oger, you say put it close to the throttle body? I thought I wanted the sensor to read at the coldest spot? Mybe I'm some were in the middle? Would engine temp. be hotter at the throttle body? But it works fine<BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723595&p=16992147&Sequence=14" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723595&p=16992147&Sequence=14</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||04-26-2000||12:54 AM||||Yes, it will set codes. Yes, you need to clear them.<P>Ed telling people to disconnect that sensor is both irresponsible and likely illegal. All ECM sensors are considered part of the emmisions system. Eliminating emissions devices usually only gains a couple HP if any, and in this case will definatly screw up the ECM function.<P>The ACT sensor is only part of the air mass calculation. Removing it will disable the real time calculations completely for all devices. The ECM may still use the O2 sensor and go closed loop but you will definatly loose all the ECM learning.<P>You want the ECM reading the temp as close to the actual temperature it will be entering the cylinders as posible.<P>As air heats it expands. Fast. As I said above engine compartment temps can be 200F+ real easy. The incoming air will heat as it goes thru the system. <P>How much heating depends on a number of things. intake plumbing. exhaust plumbing. engine comartment air flow. intake flow rate, slow air heats more since it spends more time in the intake.<P>I've watched my MAT sensor, same sensor but diferant location, it can go from 130F to below ambient, at the time about 60F, in 2-3 seconds when I punch it. No one in their right mind would believe that a 50% drop in temp didn't change the air density by a hell of alot. <P>The sensor itself is a NTC Thermistor. That mean it is a temperature sensitive resistor that drops resistance as temperature rises. (same resistor basically as in the ECM coolant sensor but diferant physically)<P>You might could partly fool the ECM by subbing a fixed value. The question is what temp are you going to fake. Fake it too far off and I can tell you that you'll very likely set more codes (44, 45 mainly) when the air temp is way off that value.<P>Anyone that doesn't think air temp changes of a few degrees can affect performance has never been near a drag strip when the clouds rolled in and screwed up everyone's engine tuning by droping air temp 20F.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jeff's '86 1/2 GT||04-26-2000||05:10 AM||||Well, theogre, I took your advice and decided to hook the sensor back up.  I mounted it right where the air box used to sit under the "new" air cleaner.  Do you think that it will be ok there?<BR>Once again, thanks for all of your insight <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000010||theogre||04-29-2000||03:22 PM||||Been sick for a few days......<P>as long as it is someplace in the intake air path it will work. I don't know what all you changed so I can't tell you the whole end result. Likely it will be fine if you used any of the aluminum tubed jobs I've seen so far. Most of those are anodized or some other bright finish that will tend to reflect heat.<P>don't forget to clear the codes from the ECM.||||reg||1
